## Overview

In this session, I learned the fundamentals of Docker and containerization, including container architecture, Docker images and containers, Dockerfiles, networking, persistent storage, image optimization, and container orchestration.

The session focused on understanding how containers provide lightweight, portable, and isolated environments for deploying modern applications.

## Topics Covered

### 1. Docker & Containerization

- Docker is a platform for packaging and running applications in lightweight containers.
- Containers package application code, dependencies, libraries, and required system tools.
- Provides consistency across development, testing, and production environments.
- Containers are lightweight and portable compared to traditional virtual machines.

### 2. Monolithic vs Microservices Architecture

#### Monolithic Architecture

- Application components are tightly coupled.
- Multiple components are deployed as a single application.
- Failure in a critical component can affect the entire application.
- Scaling individual components can be difficult.

#### Microservices Architecture

- Application is divided into smaller, independent services.
- Services can be developed, deployed, and scaled independently.
- Failure of one service does not necessarily bring down the entire application.
- Well suited for containerized environments.

### 3. Virtual Machines vs Containers

| Feature | Virtual Machines | Containers |
|---|---|---|
| OS | Each VM has a full OS | Share the host kernel |
| Size | Usually GBs | Usually MBs |
| Startup | Slower | Very fast |
| Resource Usage | Higher | More efficient |
| Isolation | Separate guest kernel | Shared host kernel |
| Portability | Relatively lower | Highly portable |

### 4. Container & Kernel Concept

- Containers share the host operating system kernel.
- This shared-kernel model makes containers lightweight.
- Linux containers can run across different environments using appropriate container runtimes and virtualization layers where required.
- Windows containers have different host and kernel compatibility requirements.

